  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "Given NNN friends, you need to find the total number of ways to pair them such that:\n\n\nEach friend can either be paired with another friend or remain single\nEach friend can be in at most one pair\nSince the answer can be large, output it modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7.\n\n\nInput Format\n\nFirst line contains an integer TTT - number of test cases\nNext TTT lines each contain a single integer NNN - the number of friends for that test case\n\n\nOutput Format\n\nFor each test case, output a single integer denoting the total number of possible pairings modulo 109+710^9 + 7109+7",
    "constraints": [],
    "inputFormat": "",
    "outputFormat": "",
    "notes": [],
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "2\n2\n3",
        "output": "2\n4",
        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1: (N=2)\nBoth remain single: (1), (2) \nThey form a pair: {1,2} \nTotal ways = 2\nTest Case 2: (N=3)\nAll remain single: (1), (2), (3) \nOne pair, one single: {1,2}, (3) \nOne pair, one single: {1,3}, (2) \nOne pair, one single: {2,3}, (1) \nTotal ways = 4"
      },
